  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to people fleeing conflict in Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Approximately 800 children under the age of 5, pregnant and nursing mothers will be provided with food rations that meet the caloric needs of one person per day.
1,000 children will receive water purifiers, and mosquito nets/blankets.
Immunization clinics, routine micronutrient supplementation, and deworming will be provided for children between 6 - 59 months of age.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Beneficiaries
Total beneficiaries:	1000
Children 0-23 months: 	650
Pregnant and lactating mothers	150
Children between 23 months and below 5 years:	2,00</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">War broke out in Sudan in Mid-April between the Sudan Armed forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) and ordinary citizens have been caught in this unprecedented power struggle. Several Attempts from international community to broker ceasefire has not fully materialized. As the war rages on, the cost of destruction of social amenities like electricity, water etc. can evidently be seen as aerial bombardment of settlements continues. As result, many people are fleeing Sudan en masse to neighbouring countries like Egypt, CHAD and South Sudan for their lives and safety as evacuation corridors were opened over the weekend of 21st April 2023. Fortunately, neighbouring countries have opened their borders to the refugee flow that is now starting. The conflict entered its deadly phase after the evacuation of international diplomats. The release of former regime leaders from Khobar prison and subsequent issuing of statement by Ahmed Haroun who is wanted by the ICC, on behalf of the leadership of the defunct regime escalated and spread the war to other regions and cities.
In South Sudan, the refugees and returnees are entering through 12 entry points and the ministry of humanitarian affairs stated that on daily basis, South Sudan is receiving an influx in average of 600 since the opening of evacuation corridors. The number could be more because of other unofficial entry routes that those fleeing would have used to enter South Sudan undocumented. One of the busiest entry ports is Renk. The number of those fleeing Sudan is growing by the day. A potential 200,000 South Sudanese are likely to return in the next 3 months while 10,000 third country nationals are expected to transit through South Sudan the next 3 months if the tensions continue.</narrative>
  </description>
